#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/c0/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/26/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119660 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/66/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/41/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)"}]}
